export const STRONG_NOTE =
  "Batch fourteen came back from the manufacturer and the pH stability data looked off. The supplier had quietly changed the preservative blend. The new system lowered the finished-product pH by about 0.4 units, enough to move the formula outside the optimal range for the emollient blend. The batch is not unsafe, but the texture changed. A same-formula reorder is not always the same formula. If you do not compare every CoA against a baseline, the customer may catch the change before you do.";

export const WEAK_NOTE = "Remind me to reorder cartons tomorrow.";

export const SAMPLE_DRAFT = `Batch fourteen of one of our formulas came back from the manufacturer with pH stability data that did not match the previous batches.

The cause was not in our formula. The supplier had changed the preservative blend without flagging it. The new preservative system lowered the finished-product pH by roughly 0.4 units. That is enough to push the formula outside the range where the emollient blend behaves the way we designed it to.

I want to be precise about what this is and is not. The batch is not unsafe. What changed was texture, and texture is exactly what a regular customer notices first.

The lesson I keep coming back to: a same-formula reorder is not always the same formula. A certificate of analysis only protects you if you compare it against a baseline every single time.

If you work with contract manufacturers, ask yourself this: when did you last compare a new CoA line by line against your first approved batch?`;
